{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792162409,"nonce":"Eq6Ssvdg0NM/mA05k7/XfaqZDlQ=","title":"Python imports every builtin declaration for every program","message":"parse_program_once ends the program with an import of the builtin library whose item list is empty, which the selective import reads as everything: all 1003 interface declarations arrive, and the checker, method typing, declaration collection and declaration lowering each walk them before the program's first statement. Lua names its import's items from the names its lowering referred to (74 declarations for an empty chunk).\n\nMeasured on the Mac (light release build, 701ca7cd, warm, ZYNTAX_TRACE_LOWER_PHASES=1) for `pass`: imports 1.06 ms, typecheck 0.87, method types 0.33, collect_decls 0.40, declarations 0.37 over 1097 declarations; compile phase 7.0 ms of about 9.3 ms to the first statement.\n\nRecording names in the Python lowering's own constructors, as Lua does, is not enough: the program also carries functions zyntax_builtins generates (per-shape dict operations and the like) whose bodies call further library functions, so 15 of the 22 speed kernels then failed with \"Call to undefined function\" (zb_slice_bound, zb_table_cap_for, zb_hash_mix, zb_dict_insert, zb_box_tuple).\n\nFix: walk the finished declarations for every name they hold (zyntax_typed_ast::names::for_each_name, exhaustive over the typed AST, types excepted) and name the import's items with them.","files":null}]}